1.3 Design principle
A plan is a finite, deterministic, acyclic structure of steps. Each step is an action from A2, a call to another plan in the library, a bounded conditional on state, or a bounded loop. Plans do not invent. They compose what already exists. The plan library is the agent's procedural ROM.


2. Plan schema
2.1 Definition 1 (Plan)
A plan p ∈ P is a tuple:
p = ⟨ id, goal_class, precondition, steps, postcondition, budget, retry_budget, version ⟩
Field
Type
Description
id
PlanID
Unique identifier (ROM-burned)
goal_class
GoalClass
The class of goals the plan serves
precondition
Predicate over S (A4)
Required starting state
steps
Sequence of Step
Finite body of the plan
postcondition
Predicate over S (A4)
Expected ending state
budget
cycles, energy
Declared worst-case resource bound
retry_budget (ρ)
Nat, at most ρ_max
Total re-attempts allowed per run (§8)
version
SemVer
Plan version (for SRAM pointer updates)
2.2 Definition 2 (Step)
Step ::=
  | ActionStep ⟨a ∈ A, params⟩
  | PlanStep   ⟨q ∈ P, params⟩
  | ConditionalStep ⟨π, then_steps, else_steps⟩
  | LoopStep  ⟨π, max_n, body_steps⟩
• π is a total, deterministic predicate over a declared read-only projection of S (A4 §5.1).
• then_steps, else_steps and body_steps are finite step sequences.
• max_n is a declared natural number, at least 1. A LoopStep repeats its body while π holds, at most max_n times.
• A PlanStep invokes a plan by id. It does not inline the callee.
• Params range over finite domains declared in the action's envelope (A2).
2.3 Authoring operators
The operators p1 ; p2 (sequence), if π then p1 else p2 (conditional) and while_bounded π max_n p (loop) are authoring-time syntax. They produce plans in the schema above. They do not exist at runtime. Runtime composition is forbidden. Every composed plan must pass admission (§5) as a plan in its own right.


3. Measures of a plan
v1.0 used one word, "length", for three different quantities. v1.1 separates them. Let steps(p) be the body of p, and let nodes in a branch or loop body count toward the enclosing plan.
3.1 Size (what ROM stores)
size counts nodes in the plan's own body. A PlanStep counts as one node and is not expanded.
size(ActionStep)  = 1
size(PlanStep)    = 1
size(Conditional) = 1 + size(then_steps) + size(else_steps)
size(Loop)        = 1 + size(body_steps)
size(sequence)    = sum of its members
3.2 Exec (worst-case executed actions in one run)
exec(ActionStep)  = 1
exec(PlanStep q)  = exec(steps(q))
exec(Conditional) = max(exec(then_steps), exec(else_steps))
exec(Loop)        = max_n × exec(body_steps)
exec(sequence)    = sum of its members
exec is an upper bound on the actions any single run executes, not the number a particular run does.
3.3 Cost (worst-case cycles in one run)
cost is defined exactly like exec, with cost(ActionStep a) = a.cost (A2) in place of 1.
3.4 Invocation depth
The invocation graph has an edge p → q whenever p contains a PlanStep naming q. Depth is the length of the longest path. Conditional and loop nesting depth within one body is counted separately.


