5. Admission: the burn-time checks
A plan enters the library only if it passes all of the following. The checks are structural and mechanical.
Check
Requirement
ADM-1
Well-formed: the plan fits the grammar of §2, and every id, predicate and parameter resolves
ADM-2
Invocation graph is acyclic with depth at most D_invoke (equivalently, a rank function exists, see §11)
ADM-3
Conditional and loop nesting depth at most D_cond
ADM-4
size(p) ≤ K_size
ADM-5
exec(p) ≤ K_exec
ADM-6
(1 + ρ(p)) × cost(p) ≤ budget(p) ≤ budget_max, and ρ(p) ≤ ρ_max
ADM-7
Every ActionStep names an action in A, with params inside that action's declared envelope
ADM-8
The plan's canonical encoding carries a valid SK-1024 signature, and its hash is recorded
The verifier that runs these checks is part of the trusted base. It must be deterministic. At the Friday session it is run by the Verifier role (White Paper §7.1) and independently re-run by the Approver.
5.1 Proposition (bounded execution)
Let P be a library whose invocation graph is acyclic. Then size, exec and cost are well-defined finite natural numbers for every plan in P. If every plan passes ADM-1 to ADM-8, then:
1.  Any single run of any plan executes at most K_exec ActionSteps.
2.  Any single run consumes at most budget(p) cycles, retries included (§8).
Proof. Order plans by the rank function of ADM-2. A plan of rank 0 contains no PlanSteps. Its body is a finite tree, so the recursions of §3 terminate on it by structural induction. A plan of rank r contains PlanSteps only to plans of lower rank, whose measures are already finite numbers by the induction hypothesis. The same structural recursion therefore terminates. For the bound, a run is a path through the body tree in which each ConditionalStep takes one branch and each LoopStep iterates at most max_n times. Each executed ActionStep is counted by exec: a conditional contributes the larger branch, a loop contributes max_n copies of its body, a sequence contributes the sum. So exec(p) is at least the number of ActionSteps executed in any run. ADM-5 gives exec(p) ≤ K_exec. The cost argument is identical, with ADM-6 for the retry factor. □
This is a proposition about admitted plans. It is only as strong as the admission check itself.


6. The plan library
6.1 Definition 3 (Plan library)
P is the ROM-burned set of plans, with m ≤ the standard maximum.
Property
Meaning
Finite
The number of plans is at most m
Indexed
Plans are indexed by goal_class for lookup. Preconditions are evaluated, not indexed
Immutable bodies
Plan bodies are ROM-burned. Only version pointers move (SRAM)
Versioned
A plan id may have several versions. One pointer selects the active version
Signed
Every version carries an SK-1024 signature over its canonical encoding
6.2 Plan lookup
Given a goal class g from GOAL_PARSE and current state s:
candidates = { p in P : goal_class(p) = g and precondition_p(s) holds }
• candidates is empty: return 0x-AMBIG-GOAL.
• candidates has one member: select it.
• candidates has several members: apply the tie-break.
The lookup considers only the active version of each plan id. Versions are resolved by the pointer, never by comparing versions across plans.
Tie-break. Among candidates with distinct ids, select by (cost ascending, id ascending). The order is total, so the same input always selects the same plan.
Strict mode. A deployment may declare strict mode, in which several candidates also return 0x-AMBIG-GOAL. Strict mode is the more conservative setting.
6.3 Activation rule UPD-1 (authority-preserving update)
The active-version pointer of plan p may move from version v to version v' only if all of the following hold:
1.  v' passes ADM-1 to ADM-8.
2.  v' is signed through the update protocol (White Paper §7).
3.  The set of actions reachable from v' is a subset of those reachable from v, and goal_class is unchanged.
A version that adds an action or changes goal_class may be activated only after the Agent Certificate (A11) is reissued and countersigned. This keeps a signed update from silently widening the agent's authority.



7. DECOMPOSE and execution semantics
7.1 DECOMPOSE
Given a selected plan p, DECOMPOSE resolves p's step tree. It is deterministic and lazy: a PlanStep is resolved when execution reaches it, and the full expansion is never materialised. Memory is bounded by at most D_invoke call frames, each holding at most K_size nodes.
Lazy expansion matters because the expanded tree can be far larger than exec(p). Dead branches and loop bodies are counted once in the tree but never all executed.
7.2 Execution
Execution interprets the step tree:
Node
Behavior
ActionStep
One sub-task
PlanStep
Resolve the callee, execute its steps, within D_invoke
ConditionalStep
Evaluate π when the step is reached, on the current state, and execute the selected branch
LoopStep
Evaluate π when reached, and again before each iteration, at most max_n times
Every sub-task:
1.  Reads its declared state projection (A4 §5.1).
2.  Executes (A1 FSM).
3.  Verifies (A5).
4.  Commits or refuses.
5.  Logs (A7).
Predicates are evaluated when reached, not at DECOMPOSE time. v1.0 evaluated them once against the starting state, which becomes stale after the first committed action.
7.3 Bound
In any run, the number of sub-task attempts is at most (1 + ρ(p)) × exec(p), where ρ(p) is the retry budget (§8).



8. Failure and rollback
8.1 Failure modes
Failure
Cause
Response
Sub-task refused
VERIFY fails
Apply the plan's failure policy
Sub-task timeout
T-AET exceeded
0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED
Invariant violated
State left I
0x-UNSAFE-ACT, halt
Precondition unmet
State drifted
0x-AMBIG-GOAL, refuse the plan
8.2 Failure policy
FailurePolicy ::=
  | Halt                 -- stop, log, refuse
  | Retry(n)             -- retry the sub-task, using the plan's retry budget
  | RollbackTo(step)     -- return to a checkpoint, using the plan's retry budget
  | Skip                 -- continue (dangerous, discouraged)
• The default policy is Halt. Every override is declared and auditable.
• Every Retry and every RollbackTo consumes one unit of the plan's retry budget ρ(p). When ρ(p) is exhausted, the plan halts.
• A re-attempt re-executes at most exec(p) actions. So a run executes at most (1 + ρ(p)) × exec(p) actions and costs at most (1 + ρ(p)) × cost(p), which ADM-6 checks against budget(p).
• Skip is allowed only where declared, and every use is logged.
8.3 Rollback
Rollback restores state to a checkpoint recorded in the append-only log (A7). Rollback is itself an action with its own envelope, and it must pass VERIFY. If the state at the checkpoint was in the invariant set I, rollback preserves I.



9. What A3 bounds and what it does not
A3 bounds: the shape of a plan, the actions a run can execute, the cycles it can consume, the invocation depth, and the memory DECOMPOSE needs. It also guarantees that no plan is created at runtime.
A3 does not bound harm. A plan of at most K_exec actions is short, not safe. Harm is limited by four other things:
1.  The action library A2: an unlisted action cannot run.
2.  The VERIFY gate A5 and the invariants of A4: an unsafe step is refused.
3.  The content of P as approved at the Friday session.
4.  Semantic verification of plans over all reachable runs (trajectory predicates, hazard matrix). This is not yet specified and belongs with A5 and the Verifiable Agency Theorem's model checking.
A3 also assumes mediation is complete. "The agent cannot invent a plan" holds only if no execution path bypasses DECOMPOSE and VERIFY. That completeness condition is outside A3.
The trust root remains. The humans who author P, and the authority that signs it, are the unverified anchors.
