11. TLA+ skeleton
This skeleton states the admission obligation for a concrete library. It has not yet been run through TLC. The argument for all libraries is the proof in §5.1. Two limits: the fuel bound combines nesting depths, so D_cond is checked by ADM-3 rather than by this module, and the encoding is a flat node table because TLA+ has no recursive record types.
---------------------------- MODULE STT1024_A3_Plans ----------------------------
EXTENDS Naturals, Sequences, FiniteSets

CONSTANTS
    A, P, Node,            \* finite sets: action ids, plan ids, node ids
    Kind,                  \* [Node -> {"action", "plan", "cond", "loop"}]
    Act,                   \* [Node -> A]        used when Kind = "action"
    Callee,                \* [Node -> P]        used when Kind = "plan"
    Then, Else, Body,      \* [Node -> Seq(Node)]  cond branches, loop body
    MaxN,                  \* [Node -> Nat]      loop bound
    Root,                  \* [P -> Seq(Node)]   top-level steps of each plan
    Owned,                 \* [P -> SUBSET Node] every node inside plan p, nested included
    ActCost,               \* [A -> Nat]
    Budget,                \* [P -> Nat]
    Rho,                   \* [P -> Nat]         retry budget
    K_size, K_exec, D_invoke, D_cond, BudgetMax, RhoMax

ASSUME /\ IsFiniteSet(A) /\ IsFiniteSet(P) /\ IsFiniteSet(Node)
       /\ K_size \in Nat /\ K_exec \in Nat
       /\ D_invoke \in Nat /\ D_cond \in Nat
       /\ BudgetMax \in Nat /\ RhoMax \in Nat
       /\ Kind \in [Node -> {"action", "plan", "cond", "loop"}]

F == (D_invoke + 1) * (D_cond + 1) + 1       \* recursion fuel

RECURSIVE Exec(_, _), ExecSeq(_, _)
Exec(n, f) ==
    IF f = 0 THEN K_exec + 1                  \* fuel exhausted counts as a violation
    ELSE CASE Kind[n] = "action" -> 1
           [] Kind[n] = "plan"   -> ExecSeq(Root[Callee[n]], f - 1)
           [] Kind[n] = "cond"   ->
                LET t == ExecSeq(Then[n], f - 1)
                    e == ExecSeq(Else[n], f - 1)
                IN  IF t >= e THEN t ELSE e
           [] Kind[n] = "loop"   -> MaxN[n] * ExecSeq(Body[n], f - 1)
ExecSeq(s, f) ==
    IF s = << >> THEN 0 ELSE Exec(Head(s), f) + ExecSeq(Tail(s), f)

RECURSIVE Cst(_, _), CstSeq(_, _)
Cst(n, f) ==
    IF f = 0 THEN BudgetMax + 1
    ELSE CASE Kind[n] = "action" -> ActCost[Act[n]]
           [] Kind[n] = "plan"   -> CstSeq(Root[Callee[n]], f - 1)
           [] Kind[n] = "cond"   ->
                LET t == CstSeq(Then[n], f - 1)
                    e == CstSeq(Else[n], f - 1)
                IN  IF t >= e THEN t ELSE e
           [] Kind[n] = "loop"   -> MaxN[n] * CstSeq(Body[n], f - 1)
CstSeq(s, f) ==
    IF s = << >> THEN 0 ELSE Cst(Head(s), f) + CstSeq(Tail(s), f)

RECURSIVE Size(_, _), SizeSeq(_, _)
Size(n, f) ==
    IF f = 0 THEN K_size + 1
    ELSE 1 + (CASE Kind[n] = "cond" -> SizeSeq(Then[n], f - 1) + SizeSeq(Else[n], f - 1)
                [] Kind[n] = "loop" -> SizeSeq(Body[n], f - 1)
                [] OTHER            -> 0)
SizeSeq(s, f) ==
    IF s = << >> THEN 0 ELSE Size(Head(s), f) + SizeSeq(Tail(s), f)

\* ADM-2: acyclic invocation graph of depth at most D_invoke,
\* equivalent to the existence of a strictly decreasing rank function.
DagWithDepth ==
    \E rank \in [P -> 0..D_invoke] :
        \A p \in P : \A m \in Owned[p] :
            Kind[m] = "plan" => rank[Callee[m]] < rank[p]

\* ADM-4 to ADM-6
Admitted(p) ==
    /\ SizeSeq(Root[p], F) <= K_size
    /\ ExecSeq(Root[p], F) <= K_exec
    /\ (1 + Rho[p]) * CstSeq(Root[p], F) <= Budget[p]
    /\ Rho[p] <= RhoMax
    /\ Budget[p] <= BudgetMax

Theorem_A3 == DagWithDepth /\ \A p \in P : Admitted(p)
=============================================================================
Theorem_A3 is what TLC checks for a concrete library at burn time. It does not need runtime model-checking.
12. Conclusion
A3 defines the plan representation that DECOMPOSE produces and EXECUTE consumes. Together with A4 it supplies the premises H2 and H3 of the Verifiable Agency Theorem.
The commitments:
1.  Plans are finite and acyclic. By construction.
2.  Plans are bounded in three declared senses: size, worst-case executed actions, and worst-case cost, enforced at admission.
3.  Plans compose at authoring time only, never at runtime.
4.  Plan updates cannot silently widen authority.
What remains open is semantic: whether the admitted plans are safe over every run they allow.
Appendix A: Plain language
What is a plan? A recipe: a bounded sequence of steps to get from here to there.
Who writes the recipes? The people who deploy the agent. They burn them into ROM after a set of mechanical checks.
Can the agent invent a new recipe? No. It selects from the ones burned in.
What if no recipe fits? It refuses with 0x-AMBIG-GOAL.
What if a step fails? The plan declares a failure policy. Retries share a small fixed budget, and when it is spent the plan halts.
Does a short plan mean a safe plan? No. A short plan is a bounded plan. Safety comes from the actions it can use, the checks every step passes, and whoever approved the recipe.
